  projected on these hosts (4 nodes x 48 cores, 100 GbE): bw 21.57 GiB/s, md 848.0 kIOPS, score 135.24
  score range over the estimated inputs: 82.7 .. 179.1
  vs WEKA Afton (score 105.94): 0.78x (bw 1.54x, md 0.40x; >1 = WEKA ahead)
  what the fixes are worth (cumulative):
      135.2  (bw  21.57 GiB/s, md   848.0 kIOPS)  as measured, valid 300 s run
      135.2  (bw  21.57 GiB/s, md   848.0 kIOPS)  + one directory's entries spread over all MDTs (name hash), finer MDT locks (done: ROCEPOOL_DIR_STRIPE=1 + MD_BUCKETS; a no-op on a run that already has them)
      135.2  (bw  21.57 GiB/s, md   848.0 kIOPS)  + FOPEN_PARALLEL_DIRECT_WRITES: no per-node inode lock on a shared file
      135.2  (bw  21.57 GiB/s, md   848.0 kIOPS)  + FUSE over io_uring (per-core queues, Linux 6.14+): ~3x per-mount rate, less wake-up cost (implemented: io500_run.py probes it against /dev/fuse on the hosts and uses the faster; a no-op on a run that already used it)
      135.2  (bw  21.57 GiB/s, md   848.0 kIOPS)  + the LD_PRELOAD interception library in every rank (no FUSE channel, no kernel on the I/O path; implemented: io500_run.py --client auto probes it against FUSE and uses the faster; a no-op on a run that already used it)
      135.2  (bw  21.57 GiB/s, md   848.0 kIOPS)  + one-sided reads and lookups (client RDMA READs stripes and MDT entries; implemented: io500_run.py --direct-read auto probes it; a no-op on a run that already used it -- its effect is in the measured per-op server CPU)
      135.2  (bw  21.57 GiB/s, md   848.0 kIOPS)  + the OST's NVMe tier for the 300 s write phases (implemented: roce/native/ost_tier.c, io500_run.py --ost-tier auto; a no-op on a run that already had it -- its limits are then the drives' capacity and write bandwidth)

